  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/27-puffers/pao-abei-formerly-tetraodon-or-monotrete-abei/",
      "html": "<p>Pao abei was scientifically described in 1998 from the Mekong river in Laos.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/27-puffers/pao-abei-formerly-tetraodon-or-monotrete-abei/",
      "html": "<p>In Southeast Asia a group of freshwater puffers exists that is extremely difficult to tell apart on a specific level. They are called the Eyespot-Puffers or the Pao-leiurus-complex. These puffers live in streams and rivers, some also in ponds and lakes, always in pure freshwater.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/27-puffers/pao-abei-formerly-tetraodon-or-monotrete-abei/",
      "html": "<p>Pao abei is among the most enigmatic species of the group. Preserved specimens are very dark, almost black, with tiny yellow or orange spots. This was exactly how the freshly collected fish looked on the photos sent by the supplier from Thailand, but the same fish look very different when kept in aquaria. This is the tricky thing with freshwater puffers: they can change their coloration very fast and very drastically. Additionally every individual has a slightly different pattern. There are only very few constant anatomical features that allow a determination, most of them overlap in the different species. So whether the recently imported fish, 7-10 cm in size, really belong to the species Pao abei is uncertain. Anyway, they are beautiful and interesting fish.</p>"
    }
  ],
